  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nTuesday-Sunday:\nThe well above normal temperatures and daily heat index values\nof 100 to 105+ degrees will be accompanied by minimal overnight\nrelief with lows in the middle to upper 70s. There is the\npotential for record temperatures through this period (see\nClimate section). The extent of the extreme heat is well \ncaptured by the NWS HeatRisk product, with widespread Major \n(3/4) to Extreme (4/4) Heat Risk over the area on Tuesday \nthrough Thursday. Other days peak into the Major (3/4) category.\nThis outlines the potential for heat- related impacts, with \nincreasing concerns as the prolonged heat envelops the entire \narea. At this time, it appears the heat wave remains locked in \nplace through at least Friday. However, ensemble guidance \nsuggests the heat persists beyond the seven day forecast period,\nwith WPC and CPC highlighting extreme heat over the region well\ninto early/mid September. NBM interquartile temperatures \nspreads are shrinking and trending towards at least the western \nhalf of the area remaining in extreme heat.\n\nCurrently, the highest heat index values of 100 to 105+ degrees\noverspread the area Tuesday through Friday. This time period is\nwhen dewpoints are progged to be highest, in the middle to upper\n60s. Confidence remains high for heat index values to remain\nbetween 100 to 105, with much lower confidence in exceeding 105. \nAny locations that do exceed 105 will generally be confined to \nnorth and west of the Ozark Plateau. Thus, a Heat Advisory \nremains the most appropriate product, though a localized\ncorridor of an Extreme Heat Warning may be warranted if \nconfidence increases.\n\nThis period of extreme heat will be dry with no mentionable \nPoPs in the forecast through much of the upcoming week. The \nextent and coverage of this heat wave will further amplify \ndrought conditions over the area, with D1 and D2 now in effect \nfor portions of southeast KS and southwest MO. CPC continues to \nhighlight concerns for a rapid onset drought. Additionally, this\ncould support an early onset of Fall fire weather season.\nContinue to monitor trends and observations.",
